#!/usr/bin/env bash
# examples — walk through common MusterAI commands, for learning/practice.
# Read-only: prints text, makes no herdr/git calls of its own.
set -euo pipefail

cat <<'EOF'
MusterAI — examples

Everything below assumes you're inside the project repo you want the agents
to work on (not this MusterAI repo) — `cd` there first, same as any other
project-scoped tool. All of these accept --dry-run: it prints exactly what
would happen (including the real herdr command) without launching claude or
creating a real git worktree, so it's the safe way to try something new.

1. Get oriented
   muster roles                              # the 11 available personas
   muster presets                            # the 4 built-in team presets

2. Spawn one agent, solo
   muster spawn data-engineer                # instance named "data-engineer"
   muster spawn data-engineer db-migration    # give it a custom instance name
   muster spawn builder "" --dry-run          # preview first — note the empty
                                               # "" placeholder when you want
                                               # to skip the name but pass flags

3. Spin up a known team (a preset)
   muster team planner-builder-tester         # general software task team
   muster team databricks-app-team my-feature # name the team yourself
   muster team data-pipeline-team --dry-run   # preview a bigger team first

4. Spin up an ad hoc team (comma-separated roles, no preset needed)
   muster team react-developer,tester-reviewer
   muster team builder,builder,tester-reviewer   # repeat a role for 2 builders
                                                   # -> named builder, builder-2

5. Watch the team (plain herdr commands — MusterAI doesn't wrap these)
   herdr agent list                           # every agent pane + live state
   herdr agent read <name>                    # peek at one agent's output
   herdr pane zoom <pane-id>                  # punch one pane to full-tab size
   herdr pane zoom --toggle --current         # zoom the pane you're in, then
                                               # run it again to un-zoom

6. Direct the team (what the orchestrator role does on your behalf)
   herdr agent prompt react-developer "start on the login form" --wait
   herdr agent prompt tester-reviewer "review the react-developer worktree" \
     --wait --until idle,done,blocked

7. Tear down when you're done
   muster stop-team my-feature                # closes the workspace, removes
                                               # worktrees; prompts first if
                                               # anything's uncommitted/unmerged
   muster stop-team my-feature --force        # skip the prompt, discard anyway

A good first exercise: from inside a real project,
   muster team planner-builder-tester practice-1 --dry-run
read the output (it shows the exact herdr + claude commands it would run),
then drop --dry-run and run it for real. `herdr agent list` afterward shows
your new team; `muster stop-team practice-1` cleans it up when you're done.
EOF
